{
  "0": "Add a residual feed-forward block after every GRU output: h' = h + W2(GELU(W1(h))), with widths 128→256→128 and W2 initialized to zero. This starts from the incumbent recurrent representation while allowing jointly trained nonlinear compatibility with item embeddings. Apply identically in training and inference across all four datasets. Preserve unit-normalized tied embeddings, history policy, final-real-position readout, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer; update the predictor writer for checkpoint compatibility.",
  "1": "Apply causal recency pooling to GRU states with weights 0.8^(t-j), normalized over observed nonpadding positions j≤t. Use pooled states at every supervised training position and select the final real pooled position at inference. Earlier evidence supports testing this readout on the current unit-normalized embedding incumbent, where the interaction remains untested. Preserve truncation, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer across all four datasets; keep predictor behavior consistent.",
  "2": "Untie history-input and target-output item embedding tables while retaining unit L2 normalization, width 128 and exactly zero padding in both. Initialize the output table as a copy of the input table, then train both jointly within each category's single GRU4Rec model. Separate embedding roles may reduce conflicting gradients between recurrent encoding and candidate discrimination. Preserve GRU topology, history policy, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer across all four datasets; update checkpoint loading and the predictor writer.",
  "3": "Use softplus(neg_score-pos_score) + 0.2*[softplus(-pos_score)+softplus(neg_score)], averaged over existing nonpadding targets. The pairwise term maintains ranking pressure while pointwise supervision constrains score drift. Earlier improvement with unnormalized embeddings motivates testing this objective on the current unit-normalized incumbent. Preserve embeddings, GRU architecture, readout, scoring, optimizer, sampling, scheduler and batch/epoch settings across all four datasets, with one checkpoint per category."
}
